    "notes": [
      "Every width in this arm is read from the landed codec; the lab proposes none of them.",
      "A landed width is still not a measured cost. No Dragon SBF program exists, so this arm carries no CU, heap, stack, account-copy, or landing figure either.",
      "Both admitted order families and a retirement share one 236-byte tagged slot, so the page holds every admitted slot kind without a second account, a second order-id chain, or a second fold.",
      "A single-Egg slot pays for the widest admitted body: 107 bytes of body under a kind byte and 128 bytes of required-zero padding. The growth from the 1819-byte v2 single-family page to 4012 is those sixteen wider slots plus one header byte; the padding is required to be zero and is not spare capacity.",
      "A cancellation writes a 80-byte tombstone into the slot and order id it retires, so a retirement never renumbers a later order and never widens the slot.",
      "place_order is pinned at the widest admitted placement, a portfolio slot at 310 bytes. It is no longer the widest intent of any kind: InitSourceSpecV2 carries a 368-byte source-spec body, so MAX_INTENT_BYTES is now 2 + HASH_BYTES + SOURCE_SPEC_BODY_V2_BYTES = 402 and a placement no longer sits at the ceiling. A single-Egg placement is 182 bytes and a padding or tombstone placement is refused by the codec at 75 bytes of header.",
      "check_count refuses outcome_count below 2 and above 16, so n=24 is a codec refusal in this arm on top of the unchanged V1 policy refusal."
    ]
